<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ruđer Bošković</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1711">1711</a>–<a href="/years/1787">1787</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Roger Joseph Boscovich, Ruggero Giuseppe Boscovich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ruđer Bošković was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> physicist, astronomer and mathematician born in Ragusa (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>). Working mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, he proposed an influential <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-theory">atomic theory</a> of point-like centres of force, measured the arc of the meridian in the Papal States and wrote on optics and astronomy. He was elected a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1761.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ruder-boskovic.jpg" alt="Ruđer Bošković" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rudjer_Boskovic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ruđer Bošković was born on 18 May 1711 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, in the Republic of Ragusa, the seventh child of the merchant Nikola Bošković and Paola Bettera, whose family had settled from Bergamo in the early 17th century. His father died when he was 10. At 8 or 9 he was sent to the local Jesuit Collegium Ragusinum, where he gained a reputation for a quick mind and an easy memory.</p><p>On 16 September 1725 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the care of two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> priests. He entered the novitiate in 1731, studied mathematics and physics, and in 1740 was appointed professor of mathematics at the Roman college. He was ordained a priest in 1744 and visited his home town only once more, in 1747.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bošković published a very large number of dissertations on subjects from the transit of Mercury and the aurora to the figure of the Earth and the theory of comets. In 1742 Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-xiv">Benedict XIV</a> consulted him, with other men of science, about a crack in the dome of St Peter's, and his suggestion of five concentric iron bands was adopted. Persuaded by the Pope to stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> rather than join a Portuguese expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, he measured with the English Jesuit Christopher Maire an arc of two degrees of the meridian between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and Rimini, begun at the end of 1750 and completed in about two years, with a map of the States of the Church.</p><p>He served Lucca as an agent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1757, and in 1760 went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> as ambassador of Ragusa to prove the republic's neutrality, a mission he completed with success; there he was elected a fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>. From 1764 he held the chair of mathematics at Pavia and directed the Brera observatory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. After the suppression of his order he accepted an invitation from the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and became director of optics for the navy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he stayed ten years. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1783 and died in Milan on 13 February 1787.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In De Viribus Vivis (1745) he sought a middle way between Newton's gravitational theory and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a>'s monad-points. His best-known work, Philosophiæ naturalis theoria redacta ad unicam legem virium in natura existentium, first published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1758, set out his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-theory">atomic theory</a> and his theory of forces. In 1753 he discovered the absence of an atmosphere on the Moon, and he devised geometric procedures for finding a planet's equator and orbit from three observations. Nietzsche's ideas of force were influenced by him, and a lunar crater and the Ruđer Bošković Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> bear his name. His nationality is claimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> alike.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roger Joseph Boscovich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The scientist <b>Ruđer Bošković</b>, born in Ragusa in 1711, became one of the leading natural philosophers of the eighteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.64,18.11&amp;year=1760" aria-label="Dubrovnik on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ruđer Bošković"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4257 3889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4257 3889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.64,18.11&amp;year=1760">What was here in 1760? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
